<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Stand Up! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Stand Up!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage in question-and-answer exchanges using simple words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can use simple words to name classroom objects they need (e.g., 'pen' or 'book'). Can express basic needs or ideas using simple phrases with teacher support (e.g., 'Open the book.' 'I need a pencil.'). Can ask and answer basic questions using simple words or gestures (e.g., 'What is it?' with 'Table' or pointing).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a "Simon Says" game using familiar classroom actions and objects. The teacher gives commands like "Simon says, 'Stand up!'" (modeling /s/ in 'says' and 'stand', stress in 'up'), "Simon says, 'Touch the desk!'" (modeling /t/ in 'touch'), "Simon says, 'Open the book!'" (modeling /b/ in 'book', /l/ in 'open'). Students follow the commands. The teacher emphasizes politeness: "Please stand up." or "Please listen." (modeling /l/ in 'listen').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of classroom objects (chair, desk, table, book, window, door) one by one and asks, "What is it?" The teacher then models the answer, "It is a [chair/desk/book]." with clear pronunciation, especially of /s/ in 'is'. The teacher also models "Please listen to the teacher," exaggerating syllable stress on 'teacher' and 'student'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses gestures and repetition.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a book) "Is this a chair? No." (Shaking head, then holding up a book again) "What is it? A book!" (Encourage choral response).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to an open door) "Is the door closed? No. It's open!" (Gesturing opening). "Open means...?" (Demonstrates opening hands).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated story or picture sequence about "A Day at School" shown on a large board or projected. Each picture shows a teacher and students interacting with classroom objects. For example: "The teacher says, 'Good morning, students! Please stand up.' A child touches the desk. The teacher says, 'Open your books.'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Following instructions (responding to commands like 'stand up', 'open the book'); Describing actions (using basic words to give an instruction, like 'Please, sit'); Responding questions (answering WH-questions like 'What is it?').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Is this story about animals or school?" (Students point to school picture). "Does the teacher say 'Hello' or 'Goodbye' in the morning?" (Students point to 'Good morning' gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (with gestures):</b> The teacher reads a statement and students show a 'thumbs up' for true or 'thumbs down' for false. "The students listen to music." (False – they listen to the teacher). "The teacher says, 'Stand up!'" (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher points to a picture and asks, "What is it?" Students point to the corresponding object in the classroom or on another flashcard (e.g., picture of a book, students point to their actual book).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Imperatives ('Stand up!', 'Open the book!', 'Listen!'), Present simple ('It is a school.', 'It is a book.'), WH-questions ('What is it?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> nouns: chair, desk, table, book, window, door, teacher, student; verbs: listen, stand up, jump, touch, open.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /s/ in 'school', 'is'; /l/ in 'listen', 'table'; Syllable stress in 'teacher', 'student'; Initial sound: /b/ 'book', /l/ 'listen', /t/ 'touch'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Choral Repetition & TPR:</b> The teacher gives commands, and students repeat chorally while performing the action. "Please stand up!" (students stand and say "Stand up!"). "Please touch the table!" (students touch table and say "Touch the table!"). "Open the book!" (students open book and say "Open the book!"). The teacher explicitly checks pronunciation of /s/, /l/, and initial sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Object Identification & Question Drill:</b> The teacher holds up various classroom objects (book, chair, desk) or points to them. The teacher asks, "What is it?" Students respond chorally "It is a [book/chair/desk]." The teacher then invites individual students to answer. Teacher focuses on 'It is a...' structure and clear articulation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Command & Respond:</b> Students work in pairs. One student (Student A) is given a flashcard with an instruction (e.g., "Stand up!", "Touch the window!", "Open the door!"). Student A says the command to Student B. Student B performs the action and responds with a simple affirmation, "Okay!" or "Yes, teacher!" (Sociolinguistic: politeness). The teacher circulates, providing support and correction, ensuring students use "Please" when giving commands.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Helpers" Role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group gets a scenario card for "It's a School Day!". One student acts as the "Teacher," and others are "Students." The "Teacher" gives simple instructions using the target vocabulary and imperatives: "Please stand up," "Touch the chair near the desk," "Open the book." "Students" respond by performing the action and can ask "What is it?" if shown an object, or simply respond "Okay, Teacher!" The teacher provides flashcards of objects or actions to prompt the "Teacher" student. The focus is on fluent interaction and completion of the commands and questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors, providing encouragement and making notes on student performance, but refrains from interrupting fluency for minor errors. Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to be the "Teacher."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Quick Check-in.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the role-play, the teacher observes students' ability to give and follow commands, and ask/answer "What is it?" questions using simple words and gestures. The teacher also uses a quick 'Show me' or 'Tell me' activity with the whole class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target words (e.g., 'book', 'listen', 'teacher', 'student') clearly enough to be understood.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 3-4 target classroom nouns and action verbs (e.g., 'chair', 'book', 'stand up', 'open').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and politeness:</b> Students give simple commands with 'Please' and respond appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students interact and respond to their peers' instructions or questions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks, students show thumbs up/down): "Can you tell a friend to 'Stand up!'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a book): "Can you say 'It is a book.'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (e.g., 'book', 'door')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to tell your friend to 'Stand up!'?" (Students can show happy/sad face).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well? The TPR activities and "Simon Says" effectively engaged students and encouraged choral and individual speaking. The "Classroom Helpers" role-play allowed for good communicative practice. Students showed good understanding of "What is it?" and imperative commands. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still need more prompting to use 'Please' and form full "It is a..." sentences; continued modeling and repetition will be helpful. Visual cues for command cards were critical. For the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), I will ensure visual aids and sentence frames are readily available to bridge the gap from speaking to writing, focusing on simple sentences like "It is a book." or "Open the door."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to verbally identify classroom objects and issue/respond to simple commands. In Lesson 4, students will use this oral vocabulary and sentence structure as a foundation to begin writing simple sentences about classroom objects and actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>